    a: "Tailor the framing, not the underlying plan: execs want strategic themes and how they ladder up to company goals/metrics, with minimal feature-level detail. Sales wants concrete, defensible talking points and realistic timing language to avoid overpromising to prospects (Now/Next/Later framing helps here specifically). Engineering wants enough detail on sequencing and dependencies to plan capacity, plus the 'why' behind priority calls they'll be asked to execute. Presenting the same overly-detailed feature list to all three either bores execs, gives sales false certainty, or gives engineering too little technical context — one artifact, several views.",
