    lvl: "med",
    q: "Explain, with a concrete example, how a positioning map (2-axis competitive analysis) can reveal a market gap that a feature-comparison checklist would miss.",
    sol: "Example axes for project-management tools: 'simplicity' (x-axis) vs 'depth of customization' (y-axis). Plotting competitors might reveal that most cluster in 'high customization, high complexity' (enterprise tools) or 'high simplicity, low customization' (consumer to-do apps), with a visibly empty quadrant — 'simple to use, but with meaningful customization for small teams.' A feature checklist comparing checkbox-by-checkbox presence of specific features wouldn't surface this — it would just show who has more checked boxes, missing the strategic whitespace visible once you plot what customers actually value along two meaningful dimensions.",
    exp: "A positioning map surfaces strategic gaps (unclaimed territory that matters to customers) that a literal feature-parity checklist, which just counts overlapping capabilities, structurally can't reveal.",
