    lvl: "easy",
    q: "A story says 'As a user, I want the database migrated to the new schema.' Explain why this fails the INVEST 'valuable' criterion, and rewrite it as a real user story (or explain what it actually is if it isn't one).",
    sol: "It fails 'valuable' because no end user directly experiences or benefits from a database migration — it's a technical task with no user-facing value on its own. It should be tracked as a technical/engineering task or sub-task, not framed as a user story; if there IS a user-facing motivation (e.g. 'migrate the schema to support faster search'), the actual user story should describe THAT user-facing outcome ('As a user, I want search results to return in under 1 second so I don't lose my train of thought'), with the migration as an implementation detail underneath it.",
    exp: "Disguising a technical task as a fake user story doesn't make it more user-centered — it just misapplies the format; some legitimate engineering work simply isn't a user story and shouldn't be forced into that shape.",
