    cat: "Delivery",
    lvl: "hard",
    q: "A stakeholder demands a hard ship date 6 months out for a feature that's still in early discovery, with nothing validated yet. Explain the risk of giving a specific date now, and propose what you'd actually commit to instead.",
    sol: "Risk: a date given before discovery is complete is effectively a guess dressed up as a commitment — if scope/approach changes once real discovery findings come in (which is likely, since nothing is validated yet), the team either blows the date or ships a compromised, under-validated version just to hit it, and either outcome damages credibility more than not giving a date at all. Propose instead: a committed date for a DISCOVERY milestone (e.g. 'we'll have validated the core approach and a confident scope/effort estimate within 6 weeks'), with the actual ship-date commitment following that milestone once there's real evidence to base it on — reframing the ask from 'commit to an outcome you don't yet have the information to promise' to 'commit to a process checkpoint that will produce a trustworthy date.'",
    exp: "Committing to a process milestone instead of a premature outcome date protects both the stakeholder relationship (they get a real, credible date eventually) and the team (they're not forced to guess or cut corners to hit an uninformed deadline).",
