    sol: "First rule out reverse causation/selection bias: check whether the type of user who adopts this feature early (e.g. power users churning for unrelated reasons, or users in a specific problematic segment/plan tier disproportionately trying it) differs systematically from non-adopters, rather than assuming the feature itself caused the worse retention. Segment the comparison by cohort/signup date and plan tier to control for that. If the pattern holds even after controlling for likely confounds, investigate whether the feature itself introduces friction/bugs/a worse experience that plausibly drives churn (support tickets, session recordings, direct interviews with adopters who later churned) — only at that point would you conclude the feature is actually net-negative, rather than a correlation mistaken for causation from raw adoption/retention numbers.",
